#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# partitions_q

#% text
# `partitions_q(n)` gives the number of partitions of `n` into distinct parts.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# By Euler's theorem this is also the number of partitions of `n` into odd parts. `partitions_q(0)` is 1.

#% item
# `partitions_q` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
partitions_q(5)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
partitions_q(0)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
partitions_q(range(10))
#% output
#= [1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10]

#% cell
partitions_q(100)
#% output
#= 444793

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The generating function, the product of `1 + q^k`:

#% cell
series(product(1 + q^k, [k, 1, 8]), q, 0, 8)
#% output
#= 1 + q + q^2 + 2*q^3 + 2*q^4 + 3*q^5 + 4*q^6 + 5*q^7 + 6*q^8 + O(q^9)

#% section
# See also

